  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "The CIA built a mining ship that mined nothing. It cost a fortune, the press photographed it, and it was a lie.",
        "visual_query": "large industrial ship open ocean",
        "visual_fallback": "industrial ship"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In March 1968 the Soviet submarine K-129 sank in the Pacific, roughly 1,560 miles north-west of Hawaii. Moscow searched and never found it.",
        "visual_query": "pacific ocean deep blue surface",
        "visual_fallback": "open pacific"
      },
      {
        "narration": "American listening stations could. The wreck sat five thousand metres down, deeper than any recovery ever attempted.",
        "visual_query": "deep ocean floor sonar darkness",
        "visual_fallback": "deep ocean"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So the cover story was Howard Hughes, mining manganese nodules off the seabed. A billionaire eccentric explains almost any strange ship.",
        "visual_query": "manganese nodules seabed mining equipment",
        "visual_fallback": "seabed nodules"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1974 the claw closed, lifted, and failed. Most of the section fell back. The agency would neither confirm nor deny.",
        "visual_query": "giant steel claw ship derrick",
        "visual_fallback": "steel claw"
      }
    ],
